<article xml:id="document" label="document">
  <title>Fall 2026 MAT 1033C Intermediate Algebra</title>

    <section xml:id="general-class-info">
    <title>What is this Course About?</title>
    <p>Hi there! My name is Anurag Katyal, and I'll be your tour guide through Intermediate Algebra-land this semester. Call me Anurag. This document covers all sections of MAT 1033C Intermediate Algebra for the Fall 2026 term — read it once carefully, because you'll be expected to know what's in it (yes, that's a hint about what's coming later).</p>

    <p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>MAT 1033C 171 — 8:00AM - 9:15AM</p></li>
      <li><p>MAT 1033C 97 — 9:30AM - 10:45AM</p></li>
      <li><p>MAT 1033C 170 — 12:30PM - 1:45PM</p></li>
    </ul>
    </p>

    <p>All sections meet Mondays, Wednesdays, and Fridays in CB 202 on the Boca Raton campus, from 08/21/2026 to 12/09/2026.</p>

    <p>
      This course prepares you for College Algebra (MAC1105) by working through five families of functions: linear, absolute value, polynomial, rational, and radical.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>This course has no in-person lectures — and that's the point.</alert> We run in a flipped, active-learning format: you do the Pre-Class Prep (PCP) <em>before</em> class on your own time, and class time is reserved for working problems in groups with me circulating to help. If you're hoping to sit back and watch someone lecture at a whiteboard, this is the wrong class. If you're willing to show up having done the reading and dig in with your group, you'll do great.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="instructor-info">
    <title>How do I get in touch with Anurag?</title>
    <p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Name: Anurag Katyal</p></li>
      <li><p>Discord: <url href="https://discord.gg/48scr9d5Ab" visual="Join Server"/> (fastest way to reach me — I live here)</p></li>
      <li><p>Email: <url href="mailto:katyala@pbsc.edu" visual="katyala@pbsc.edu"/></p></li>
      <li><p>Office Location: CB 0224</p></li>
    </ul>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="textbooks-materials">
    <title>What do I need for Class?</title>
    <p>Good news for your wallet: no textbook, no access code. We use free, open-source materials all semester.</p>
    <p>You will still need to show up with:</p>
    <p>
    <ul>
      <li>A laptop with administrative rights, running Windows or Mac OS. Bring it to every class meeting.</li>
      <li>Not a Chromebook, not a tablet — those don't run what we need. See above: laptop.</li>
      <li>A scientific calculator — specifically the TI-30XIIS. It runs about $15 online (Walmart/Amazon/Target); the bookstore sells it too, at a markup you don't need to pay.</li>
      <li>Two 70-page spiral-bound notebooks.</li>
    </ul>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="time-commitment">
  <title>How Much Time Should I Expect to Spend Outside Class?</title>
  <p>Budget a minimum of 3 hours of outside work per hour of class time — about 12 hours a week. If you're repeating the course, aim higher: 14-16 hours a week. Algebra doesn't care how busy your schedule is.</p>
</section>

  <section xml:id="learning-targets">
    <title>What Are Learning Targets, and How Do I Earn Them?</title>
    <p>Instead of one high-stakes test per topic, this course uses mastery-based grading built around 16 Learning Targets (LTs): 10 are marked <alert>CORE</alert> (non-negotiable prerequisites for College Algebra), 5 are elective content targets, and 1 (LT16) is earned through attendance rather than content. Every LT is graded proficient (1) or not-yet (0) — no partial credit, no partial credit, did we mention no partial credit?</p>

    <p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>(LT 1) Explain and justify what it means for a number or object to be a solution to an equation or inequality or their systems.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 2, CORE) Formulate, solve, and interpret linear equations, and evaluate solutions in context.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 3) Determine, represent, and interpret equations of lines, and analyze how slopes and intercepts describe relationships.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 4, CORE) Analyze and solve absolute value equations, represent their solutions, and justify reasoning.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 5, CORE) Model, construct, and solve systems of linear equations from word problems, and evaluate the reasonableness of solutions.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 6) Solve, represent, and interpret linear inequalities in one and two variables, and compare different forms of solution sets.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 7, CORE) Apply and evaluate the properties of exponents to simplify expressions, and classify and construct polynomials by degree and number of terms.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 8) Perform, verify, and connect operations on polynomials to broader algebraic structures.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 9, CORE) Select, apply, and justify appropriate factoring techniques for polynomials.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 10, CORE) Construct and solve equations by factoring, and evaluate solutions for accuracy and context.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 11) Perform, simplify, and analyze operations on rational expressions.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 12, CORE) Construct, solve, and interpret rational equations, and evaluate potential restrictions on solutions.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 13, CORE) Perform, simplify, and justify operations on radical expressions.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 14, CORE) Construct and solve radical equations, and evaluate solutions to identify extraneous roots.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 15, CORE) Perform and justify operations on complex numbers and analyze and solve quadratic equations with complex solutions.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 16) Marked absent six or fewer times throughout the semester (see <xref ref="attendance-policy"/>).</p></li>
    </ul>
    </p>

    <p><alert>The retake system is generous, so use it.</alert> Each LT is assessed on regular quizzes (about 20 over the semester, 6-20 minutes each), and you get 3 attempts to reach proficiency on each one. Once you hit proficient, you're done — no need to retake for style points. You can retake up to 4 quizzes per week, so if Tuesday's quiz on linear inequalities doesn't go your way, there's room for a comeback later that week. The exception: LT14 and LT15 don't get the normal retake treatment — your one remaining shot at those two is the final exam (see <xref ref="final-exam"/>).</p>

    <p>All work must be shown on every assessment to earn credit. Grading rewards correct, clear, logical presentation with proper units, notation, and labeled functions — a right answer with no work, or a mess of one, does not earn credit. If I can't follow your reasoning, neither can you, six months from now.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="final-exam">
    <title>What's the Deal with the Final Exam?</title>
    <p>The final is cumulative and given in two parts: Monday 12/07/2026 and Wednesday 12/09/2026. Both parts are mandatory — missing either one, or scoring below 35% overall, is an automatic F for the course, no exceptions. This is the one absolute hard line in the grading system, so don't test it.</p>

    <p>Beyond that floor, the final does double duty:</p>
    <p>
    <ul>
      <li>It's your only remaining shot at LT14 and LT15, if you haven't already earned them.</li>
      <li>Its score nudges your Learning-Target-earned letter grade up or down, as follows:</li>
    </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>85% or above: grade goes up by up to 2 letter grades.</p></li>
      <li><p>70%-84.99%: grade goes up by up to 1 letter grade.</p></li>
      <li><p>60%-69.99%: no change.</p></li>
      <li><p>50%-59.99%: grade goes down by up to 1 letter grade.</p></li>
      <li><p>35%-49.99%: grade goes down by up to 2 letter grades.</p></li>
      <li><p>Below 35%, or a missed part: automatic F, overriding everything above.</p></li>
    </ul>
    </p>

    <p>The idea here is balance: the retake system all semester gives you plenty of room to recover from a rough week and build a solid Learning-Target grade on your own terms. The final can't wipe that out over one bad morning — the swing is capped at two letter grades either direction, except for the hard floor above, which exists because a cumulative score that low means something more fundamental didn't click, no matter how the semester's quizzes went. Conversely, a genuinely strong final can boost you — but it won't turn a semester of not-yet's into an A. Translation: consistent effort all semester is still the surest path, and the final is a meaningful checkpoint, not a lottery ticket in either direction.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="grading">
    <title>How Is My Letter Grade Calculated?</title>
    <p>Your letter grade is based on how many CORE and elective (non-CORE) Learning Targets you've earned by the end of the semester — this is your grade <em>before</em> the final exam adjustment described in <xref ref="final-exam"/> is applied.</p>

    <tabular>
      <col width="15%"/>
      <col width="85%"/>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Grade</cell>
          <cell>Requirement</cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell>A</cell><cell><p>10 CORE LTs and 4, 5, or 6 elective LTs</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell>B</cell><cell><p>10 CORE LTs and 2 or 3 elective LTs</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell>C</cell><cell>
          <p>10 CORE LTs and 0 or 1 elective LT, or</p>
          <p>9 CORE LTs and 4, 5, or 6 elective LTs, or</p>
          <p>8 CORE LTs and 5 or 6 elective LTs</p>
          </cell></row>
        <row><cell>D</cell><cell>
            <p>9 CORE LTs and 0-3 elective LTs, or</p>
            <p>8 CORE LTs and 0-4 elective LTs</p>
          </cell>
          </row>
        <row><cell>F</cell><cell><p>7 or fewer CORE LTs mastered</p></cell></row>
      </tabular>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="attendance-policy">
  <title>What is the Attendance Policy?</title>

    <p><alert>Attendance on the first day of class is mandatory.</alert> Miss it and you may be dropped for non-attendance. Beyond that, there are no excused or unexcused absences — I don't need to know why you're out, and I won't ask; I consider that your business. Share if you want to talk it through, but you're never required to.</p>

    <p>You're marked absent for a class meeting if you:</p>

    <p>
    <ul>
      <li>don't complete the PCP to at least 90% completion,</li>
      <li>aren't present when attendance is taken at the start of class (there are no tardies — you're either there or you're not),</li>
      <li>leave before class is dismissed,</li>
      <li>use an electronic device when it isn't permitted, or</li>
      <li>use an electronic device for anything unrelated to class.</li>
    </ul>
    </p>

    <p>Six or fewer absences earns you LT16 (see <xref ref="learning-targets"/>). More than six, and LT16 is out of reach for the semester — no retakes on attendance you didn't do. Rack up 10 absences and you'll be withdrawn from the course entirely (see <xref ref="withdrawal-policy"/>).</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="withdrawal-policy">
    <title>What is the Withdrawal Policy?</title>
    <p>Withdrawal can happen one of two ways:</p>
    <p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>I withdraw you: if you're marked absent for a 10th class meeting, you're automatically withdrawn for excessive absences — see <xref ref="attendance-policy"/>.</p></li>
      <li><p>You withdraw yourself: the last day to withdraw with a "W" is 2026-10-27. It's on you to file the withdrawal request through Workday; once processed, you'll receive a "W" for the course.</p></li>
    </ul>
    </p>
    <p>Note that withdrawing to dodge a failing grade for academic dishonesty isn't an option — see <xref ref="academic-dishonesty-policy"/>.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="late-assignment-policy">
    <title>What is the Late Submission/Make-Up Policy?</title>
    <p>Short version: there isn't one. No late submissions and no make-ups for any graded assignment. If you miss a quiz for any reason, that attempt is gone, but you keep your eligibility for a retake — the generous retake system already built into this course is the make-up policy.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="academic-dishonesty-policy">
    <title>What is the Academic Dishonesty Policy? What happens if I am Caught Cheating?</title>
    <p>Academic dishonesty results in a failing grade for this course, full stop. And no, withdrawing afterward to dodge that grade doesn't work either (see <xref ref="withdrawal-policy"/>). Do the work as your own — it's not just an ethics thing, it's the only way any of this actually sticks.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="accessibility-statement">
    <title>Accessibility Statement</title>
    <p>Students with disabilities are advised, in compliance with federal and state laws, that accommodations and services are available through the Center for Student Accessibility (CSA). It is the student's responsibility to contact CSA advisors and submit appropriate documentation prior to receiving accommodations or services.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="student-code-of-conduct">
    <title>How Do I Actually Do Well in This Course?</title>

    <p>Do this:</p>

    <p>
    <ol>
      <li>Keep a consistent sleep schedule — at least 7 hours a night. Sleep-deprived brains are bad at algebra, and at most things.</li>
      <li>Leave early enough to arrive 10 minutes before class starts. Traffic-induced panic and factoring polynomials don't mix.</li>
      <li>Finish the PCP at least 36 hours before it's due — not the night before. That buffer is what lets you ask questions and actually absorb the material before class uses it.</li>
      <li>Participate in group work and stay engaged. Phones and other distractions go away for the duration of class.</li>
      <li>Speak up. Share your solutions loudly and clearly when it's your turn — half of learning this material is explaining it out loud.</li>
      <li>Do the homework the same day the topic is covered, while it's still fresh enough to ask questions about.</li>
      <li>Ask and answer questions on Discord — the retake system rewards people who stay engaged between classes, not just during them.</li>
      <li>Check in with me <em>before</em> things get difficult, not after.</li>
    </ol>
    </p>

    <p>Don't do this:</p>

    <p>
    <ol>
      <li>Show up late.</li>
      <li>Start the PCP late at night, to the point where you can't learn from it or barely finish before it's due.</li>
      <li>Stay quiet and let your group carry the conversation.</li>
      <li>Hold back an answer because you're afraid it's wrong — wrong answers are how this class actually works.</li>
      <li>Start the homework at the last minute and either abandon it or let an AI tool do it for you while you learn nothing. It will not be on your side during the quiz.</li>
      <li>Go dark on Discord when you're stuck.</li>
      <li>Disappear instead of telling me what's getting in your way. I don't have a magic wand, but I do have office hours and a functioning retake system — neither helps if I don't know there's a problem.</li>
    </ol>
    </p>

  </section>

</article>
